          %p When both left and right rules are shown, we only need one of them if we have Symmetry, but we need both if we lack Symmetry (or if we are using proximal neighbourhoods in constructive mathematics; see below). Even so, Isotony is usually given on both sides, since it is convenient to combine both directions into a single statement. On the other hand, Isotony is equivalent to the converse of binary Additivity, so sometimes these are combined instead (so Isotony does not explicitly appear), usually on only one side when Symmetry is used.
          %p Whether made explicit or not, Isotony is very fundamental. In particular, it allows us to assert Reflexivity only for singletons, although this is often not done (to avoid mentioning points).
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ll
            is unreasonably strong (as is binary Additivity for
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            because the other axioms for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            seem unreasonably strong constructively, while Decomposability is automatic in classical mathematics.)
          %p
            A
            %strong topogeny
            is a relation that satisfies both forms of Isotony and all four forms of Additivity. A
            %strong topogenous relation
            or
            %strong quasipreproximity
            is a topogeny that also satisfies Reflexivity; a
            %strong quasiproximity
            is a quasipreproximity that also satisfies Reflexivity and Transitivity. A topogeny is
            %strong symmetric
            if it satisfies Symmetry; a
            %strong preproximity
            is a symmetric quasipreproximity, and a
            %strong proximity
            is a symmetric quasiproximity. A topogeny is
            %strong separated
            if it satisfies Separation. A topogeny is
            %strong perfect
            if it satisfies left Perfection,
            %strong coperfect
            if it satisfies right Perfection, and
            %strong biperfect
            if it satisfies both; a symmetric topogeny is usually called simply
            %strong perfect
            if it satisfies any form of Perfection, because then it must satisfy both (except in constructive mathematics using proximal neighbourhoods). Constructively, a proximity space satisfying Regularity may be called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong proximally regular
          %p
            A
            %strong (quasi)-(pre)-proximity space
            is a set equipped with a (quasi)-(pre)-proximity. All of these terms may be used with
            = succeed "," do
              %em nearness
            = succeed "," do
              %em apartness
            = succeed "," do
              %em proximal neighbourhoods
          .un_remark
            %h6#warnings Warnings
            %p
              Some authors require a (quasi)-proximity to be separated; conversely, some authors do not require a (quasi)-proximity to satisfy Transitivity (so ‘pre’ is the default for them). The term ‘topogeny’ is also not found in the literature (except
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/toddtrimble/published/topogeny"} here
              = succeed "\u2019," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topogenous+relation"} topogenous relation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntopogenous+spaces"} syntopogenous spaces
              for a quasipreproximity. The terminology for Perfection also comes from syntopogenous spaces.
            %p
              Transitivity is sometimes called
              = succeed "," do
                %em normality
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+space"} normal space
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separation axiom
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+regular+space"} complete regularity
              than to normality, and at least in classical mathematics every proximity space is completely regular — but this is not true in constructive mathematics, or for quasi-proximity spaces, so it is debatable whether it should be attributed to the Transitivity axiom. When comparing proximities to topologies via
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntopogenies"} syntopogenies
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopological+space"} pretopological space
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are (quasi)-(pre)-proximity spaces, then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
            is said to be
            %strong proximally continuous
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi δ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \;\delta\; B
            implies
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi δ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*(A) \;\delta\; f_*(B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⋈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \bowtie B
            whenever
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋈
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*(A) \bowtie f_*(B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≪
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*(C) \ll f^*(D)
            whenever
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ≪
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \ll D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi QProx
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QProx
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prox
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prox
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functors"} forgetful functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi QProx
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QProx \to Set
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Prox
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prox \to Set
            (taking a space to its set of points) make them into
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+concrete+categories"} topological concrete categories
          %h3#in_constructive_mathematics In constructive mathematics
          %p
            As remarked above, the strong Symmetry axiom for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ll
            is too strong in constructive mathematics. In fact, if it is satisfied by a nontrivial space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            follows: for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg P
            is true, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=X
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = \{ x\in X \mid P \}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ≪
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^{\mathsf{c}} = \emptyset \ll \emptyset = A^{\mathsf{c}}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ll B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\subseteq B
            and so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            holds.
          %p
            On the other hand, while the Symmetry axioms for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bowtie
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            may seem stronger than the allowable weak symmetry axiom for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ll
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ll
            (and weak Symmetry), if we define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⋈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie B
            to mean
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≪
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ll B^{\mathsf{c}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bowtie
            implies that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⋈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie B
            then
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi c
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie (B^{\mathsf{c}})^{\mathsf{c}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bowtie
            is completely determined by its restriction to subsets that are complements.
          %p
            In this sense, a proximal neighborhood space actually contains more information than a proximal apartness space; it is unclear whether the latter gives rise to the former constructively. On the other side, we can of course define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi δ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\;\delta \; B
            to mean
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋈
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg(A\bowtie B)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            seems too strong: it is apparently not satisfied even by a metric space.
          %p We can, however, show the following constructively.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p (Quasi-)proximal apartness spaces satisfying Decomposability are equivalent to (quasi-)proximal neighborhood spaces satisfying Decomposability.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              As above, in one direction we define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie B
              to mean
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≪
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ll B^{\mathsf{c}}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ll B
              to mean that there exists a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie C
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi C
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\cup C = X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≪
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ll
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ll B
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≪
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ll C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋈
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie E
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\cup B = X
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi C
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\cup C = X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋈
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie D\cup E
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (D\cup E) \cup (B\cap C) = X
              by distributing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cup
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cap
            %p
              To prove Decomposability (and hence Transitivity) for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≪
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ll
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≪
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ll B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie C
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi C
                      %mo =
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\cup C = X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bowtie
              we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D,E
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⋈
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\bowtie D
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
